1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A router does one small thing per packet: look up the destination and send it to one neighbour.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Building that table is routing, and there are two honest ways to do it.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Distance vector knows only its neighbours. Link state knows the whole graph.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Distance vector runs Bellman-Ford. Link state runs Dijkstra.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The whole of distance vector is one line: the best route through any neighbour.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
GATE CSE 2021 Set 2 gives R three neighbours and asks for its new table.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Eight is the cost, and it came through Y, so Y is the next hop.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
For Q the three sums are 7, 8 and 13, so the cost is 7 through X.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
GATE CSE 2011 gives five stabilised vectors and then changes one link.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The link N2 to N3 drops to 2. N3 keeps only its neighbours N2, N4 and N5.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The new row at N3 is 3, 2, 0, 2, 5 — option A of GATE CSE 2011.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Then N1 to N2 fails, and the next round gives N3 a cost of 10 to N1.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A network hangs off B. A reaches it in 2, B in 1. Then that link dies.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Before B can tell A, A advertises 2. B now believes 3 through A.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Then A hears 3 and takes 4, B hears 4 and takes 5, and it climbs by one each time.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
RIP makes 16 mean infinity, so the climb stops at 16 and the route dies.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Split horizon: never advertise a route back out of the interface you learned it from.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Poison reverse says the same thing out loud: advertise it back, but as infinity.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Triggered updates and hold-down timers cut the delay that the loop feeds on.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
With three routers in a line, split horizon still loops: the page simulates it.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A link state router describes only itself: its links and their costs.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Those advertisements are flooded, so every router ends with the same map.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Each router runs Dijkstra with itself as the root, on that same map.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The price is messages: flooding costs more than telling your neighbours.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Dijkstra keeps a set of settled routers and a tentative cost for the rest.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Take the unsettled router with the smallest tentative cost, then relax its links.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
On six routers the settled order is A, C, B, D, E, F with costs 0, 2, 3, 8, 10, 13.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The result is a tree, and the tree is what a routing table is made from.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The next hop is the first step from the root along the tree, not the last.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Distance vector, started from scratch on the same graph, reaches the same six numbers.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Both are checked against an independent all-pairs computation.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So the choice is never about the final answer. It is about knowledge, messages and time.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
RIP is distance vector in hops, carried in UDP on port 520, capped at 15 hops.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
OSPF is link state with a cost metric, carried directly in IP protocol 89.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Both are interior gateway protocols. Neither is used between domains.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Sort every protocol fact into five slots and the one-mark questions become recall.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: is the question about a distance vector update, a Dijkstra run, or protocol facts?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: for a vector update, use only the neighbours and only the previous round.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: for a Dijkstra question, build the graph, then run extract-min, then read the first hop.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Step four: check the answer against the traps, then against the size of the numbers.

